A||||7||wahoo4u||up in smoke||||9||
Z||000000||wahoo4u||01-10-2001||09:59 PM||||any help is much appreciated.  I was driving home the other night an my worst nightmare came true.  I stopped at a stop sign looked in my rearview mirror and the back end of my car had disapeared in smoke.  Don't know what happened.  I have an 86 SE v6.  For some reason I am leaking oil (don't know location as of yet) plus to make matters worse, I am buring oil just as fast as it is leaking.  With in about the seven miles i drove it like this, I ran really low on oil.  It has a small knock at this point, I presume from low oil level.  Had good oil pressure the entire time.  Major loss of power though.  Any ideas as to what may have happened i would love to hear.  I don't want to lose this car.  I love it to much.  What would be cheaper at this point?  Engine swap, engine fix, or new engine?  Up for anything right now.  Thanx for any info.<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000001||DJRice||01-10-2001||10:21 PM||||Nothing good happened, thats for sure.  Your oil likely leaked on the cat. converter or exhaust manifold.  If that is the case, you are very lucky you did not end up with a fire on your hands.  As for the oil leak, it could have come from any number of places.  You could have bad head gasket or the oil filter could be loose.  The only way to tell will be to investigate the mess thats left over.  <P>As for the power loss, well, you may need to start thinking about an engine rebuild or swap, because considering the oil leak and smoke, you have ruined the engine.  <P><P>------------------<BR>Dillon<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000002||My7Fieros||01-10-2001||10:35 PM||||DJs right, def not a good thing that happened to your engine, especially with the loss of power. There are shields over most of the exhaust component to help prevent the oil from catching fire when landing on them..that may have been your saving grace.<BR>As far as where the leak is from...hard to say. I would <I>suspect</I> that its from the upper part of the engine. Oil leaks from the lower parts like a leaky oil pan tend not to drip on anything except the ground. Take a look at the engine, I would think it would be obvious. The fact that it happened suddenly is suspicious...something def sh1t itself! Hard to say right now...take a look at the engine when you get a chance and reply here with where you think the oil is comming from.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||01-10-2001||10:42 PM||||When you check for leaks, be sure to pay attention to the tube that goes to the oil sending unit. Possiblity exists the there is a pin hole in it which is spewing oil all over the place.||||reg||1
Z||000004||tgowens||01-11-2001||12:53 AM||||if the leak appears to be on the left side of the engine I would suspect the distributor o-ring. If it is on the right side I would suspect the sending unit......||||reg||1
Z||000005||wahoo4u||01-12-2001||02:24 PM||||Somewhat of an update.  Have not found a definate leak as of yet.  The intake has oil on it around the distributer.  The engine is only a quart low on oil so I am sure that the knock is not a good thing.  The truck side of the block is covered in oil as well as the oil pan.  As far as i can tell the other side of the block is try yet it appears that oil was splattered on the firewall.  But with leaking oil, it can go anywhere.  Still looking and hoping for the best.  As of right now, considering upgrading to a bigger engine.  Thanks for the ideas.<P>||||reg||9
Z||000006||88formula||01-12-2001||06:33 PM||||Maybe the oil line that leads up to the oil pressure sending unit has rusted and ruptured and the oil is spraying out from there. Just a thought!<P>Th o-ring around the distributor may be leaking too.||||reg||1
Z||000007||hugh||01-12-2001||08:12 PM||||In order to find a leak you need to first clean the engine of all that oil.Blow it dry or at least let it air dry for a while.Then check it thoroughly to make sure there is no oil anywhere on it.Then run it for a minute or two,shut it off and reinspect it for any oil.If you find any,clean it off again and repeat the procedure.If you have oil again at the same spot,that's your leak.From what you described,it should be relatively easy to find your leak.You can use Brakeclean to clean hard to get at spots,it is a pressurized can and can be gotten at any auto parts store.Good luck.  <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/hugh_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
